        "message": "That points to a gap between *recognizing* the material while reading and *generating* it under pressure. Here are three ways to target that directly for next time: 1. **Jot before you write (in the exam).** When you hit a written prompt, don't try to write complete sentences right away. Quickly sketch out raw bullet points, keywords, or a quick outline in the margin. Getting any words on paper lowers the pressure and breaks the freeze.\n2. **Switch from passive review to timed prompts (while studying).** Re-reading chapters builds familiarity, but written tests require recall. Turn chapter headings into questions and practice writing timed 3-to-5-minute answers without looking at your notes.\n3. **Go over one written answer with your instructor.** Ask them to look at where your response broke down—whether it was missing specific terminology, lacking structure, or incomplete arguments. When you froze, was it that your mind went completely blank, or did you know what you wanted to say and couldn't get it organized?",
